    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Rates can now carry a from-date, and all 4 acceptance tests pass with `bin/e2e`.\n\n**Stored data**\n- A new migration, `V10__rate_from_date.sql`, adds a `from_date` column (empty means \"from the beginning of time\"). The old one-rate-per-grade uniqueness is replaced by one rate per grade and from-date.\n- The database doesn't treat two empty from-dates as duplicates, so the code enforces that instead. It looks up the existing rate for that grade and from-date and replaces it, with the sheet locked so two settings of the same rate happen one at a time.\n\n**Server**\n- Setting a rate takes an optional `from` and replaces any rate with the same grade and from-date.\n- The `RATE_SET` audit line gets ` from=<date>` at the end, only when a from-date was given.\n- Settling a claim uses the rate in force on the claim's date: the one with the latest from-date on or before it. If there isn't one, due is stored as n/a, as before for a grade with no rate.\n\n**Front end**\n- The rate form has a from-date field (`rate-form-from`).\n- An \"In force today\" table shows `rate-current-<grade>`: the rate in force on the sheet's today, or n/a if there is none.\n- An \"All rates\" table lists every rate as `rate-row-<grade>-<from|start>` with `rate-pct`.\n- A claim's due uses the rate in force on the claim's date. A claim dated before every rate for its grade shows n/a.\n\n**Decision for you:** a person's due-total, and the sheet's due-total, still multiply what they've claimed by the rate in force **today**. They don't add up the dues of their individual claims, so the two can differ. In the first test, Ada's due-total is 9.00 while her claims' dues add up to 7.00. I kept it this way because the tests expect those numbers.\n\n**Visible change:** the `rate-row-<grade>-start` rows now appear only in the \"All rates\" table, not in the per-grade table they used to share with `rate-current-<grade>`. The test IDs themselves are unchanged.",
